WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
23 million metric tons of road salt spread each winter now, Salonize 44% of Northeastern

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:12.920
Lakes. This is unintended consequences episode 169. This is the story of a 1938 safety choice

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.560
on New Hampshire pavement. The way that choice scaled after the war, the chemistry

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:23.260
it left in lakes, pipes, and wells, the partial fixes agencies are testing now, and the missing

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:28.860
measurement that still sits beside the crash count. In 1938, highway crews in New Hampshire

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.780
tried spreading sodium chloride on state roads to keep them clear through winter.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.180
The chloride ions never degrade.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:41.820
A 2017 analysis of 371 North American lakes found that those sampled in the Midwest and

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.680
Northeast were steadily gaining salt.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.240
The practice began to reduce crashes on icy pavement.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.080
It has instead changed the chemistry of thousands of lakes and streams across the continent.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.280
Highway engineers in New Hampshire were facing rising winter accident rates on the new paved

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.240
roots of the late 1930s. They knew bear pavement let traffic move at normal speeds and cut

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:08.300
the number of spinouts and collisions. State records show the first experimental applications

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:15.900
in 1938 and a full statewide policy by the winter of 1941-42. At the time, the only measured

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.860
cost was the price of the salt itself. No one tracked what happened to the dissolved chloride

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:26.060
once it left the roadway. The decision rested on the same logic that later guided the interstate

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:31.700
highway system. Keep vehicles moving safely through every season. Engineers compared Salt

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.740
directly with the older practice of spreading sand or cinders. Sand and cinders left a

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:41.860
gritty residue that slowed traffic and had to be put down again after each storm. Salt

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.740
dissolved and left the surface visibly dry, so one pass could last through several hours

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.960
of light snowfall. They also weighed the human cost of winter crashes, which climbed each

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:57.440
year as more cars use the expanding network of two-lane paved roads that froze quickly after sunset.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:02.260
Because the salt came from nearby mines and could be stockpiled in simple sheds,

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.780
the upfront expense looked modest next to lost work days and damaged vehicles.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.280
No agency yet collected baseline chloride readings and streams or wells,

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:16.900
so the engineers had no data suggesting the material would persist. Their focus stayed on the

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:22.360
immediate, observable result, fewer calls to tow trucks and fewer reports of Jackknife trucks

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:27.760
blocking the main routes into Concord and Manchester. After World War II, states across the

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.960
Northeast and Midwest adopted the same approach as traffic volumes grew, and drivers came to

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:39.500
expect clear pavement within hours of any storm. Rocks salt used client from roughly 160,000

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:46.720
tons in the early 1940s to several million tons by the 1960s. The American Geosciences Institute

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:53.680
later recorded that annual U.S. roadway deicing consumption reached 24.5 million metric tons in 2014.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.880
Early reports praised the drop in winter fatalities and the smoother flow of commerce.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.440
A few limonologists noted rising chloride readings in nearby streams,

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.020
but those measurements stayed in technical journals and did not alter maintenance contracts.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.060
Departments of transportation wrote performance standards that measured success by the number

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.460
of lane miles returned to bear pavement within a set number of hours after a storm.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.540
Those standards rewarded crews who applied salt early and heavily.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:26.880
As the interstate highway system opened long stretches of limited access rode, maintenance

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.780
supervisors found that salt kept those high-speed corridors open when sanding alone could

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.820
not prevent ice build-up on bridge decks.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.040
Municipal budgets shifted toward larger spreader trucks and heated storage domes that kept

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.540
salt dry and free-flowing.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:51.580
By the 1970s, private contractors bidding on state winter maintenance jobs competed on how quickly they could achieve bear pavement, reinforcing the same chemical choice year after year.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:58.200
Chloride does not break down or settle out. Every ton applied eventually reaches groundwater, streams or lakes.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:07.780
The 2017 paper in the proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, led by Hillary Dugan, examined long-term records from 371 lakes,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.840
and found that impervious cover as low as 1 percent of a lake's watershed was enough

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.740
to drive measurable salinization.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:21.300
The authors estimated that roughly 7,770 lakes across the region could exceed the environmental

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:27.500
protection agency aquatic life criterion of 230 milligrams per liter within 50 years.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:35.180
A companion 2018 paper in the same journal by Suje Kauschel analyzed 232 United States Geological

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:41.700
river sites and documented salinization across 37% of the contiguous US drainage area,

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:45.560
with road salt the dominant source in New England and the mid-Atlantic.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:51.640
The added ions also raise alkalinity, corrode drinking water pipes, and alter the seasonal

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:57.460
mixing of deeper lakes, reducing oxygen for cold water fish, invertebrate communities shift

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:02.500
towards salt tolerant species, and the taste of municipal supplies changes in cities that

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.140
draw from affected watersheds.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:09.180
These effects unfold over decades because the residents' time of chloride in many lakes

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.460
exceeds 50 years.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.860
Once salt reaches a lake, the denser saline water sinks and weakens the spring and fall

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:18.580
turnover that normally mixes oxygen to the bottom.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:24.040
Without that mixing, phosphorus released from sediment stays in the hypolymneon and fuels

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.940
algal blooms the following summer.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.460
Pipep corrosion accelerates when the same sodium and chloride ions increase the conductivity

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:36.100
of finished drinking water, allowing lead and copper to leach from older service lines.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:41.640
In rural areas where private wells draw from shallow aquifers, chloride concentrations

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.900
have climbed steadily, forcing some homeowners to install reverse osmosis systems or haul

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:47.940
water for livestock.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:54.340
The same ions travel downstream and reach tidal estuaries, where they alter the osmotic balance

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:59.240
for juvenile fish and shellfish that spend part of their life cycle in low salinity zones.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:05.300
Transportation agencies have begun testing pre-wedding, Brian solutions, and pavement sensors

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:10.620
that cut salt application rates by 20-40% while maintaining safety standards.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:15.380
Some municipalities now track salt use by truck and fine excessive spreading.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:21.300
The same ions that once seemed harmless continue to move through aquifers, so lakes already

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.360
enriched will take generations to recover even if applications stop tomorrow.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:30.760
Researchers at the Carey Institute note that fresh water salinization syndrome now links

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:36.900
road salt, fertilizers, and wastewater across entire river basins, complicating any single

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:37.600
source fix.

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:41.040
No large-scale reversal of past loading has yet occurred.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:46.660
Pilot programs that substitute magnesium chloride or calcium chloride for sodium chloride

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.140
reduce the sodium load but still deliver chloride ions that do not degrade.

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:57.920
Water shed groups have mapped salt-vulnerable lakes and asked towns to designate reduced salt zones near those shores.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.880
Yet enforcement remains voluntary because state highway departments retain authority over the largest roads.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:10.740
Long-term monitoring networks continue to record rising baseline chloride levels even in years of lighter snowfall,

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:17.600
showing that legacy salt stored in soil and groundwater keeps entering surface waters long after any single winter ends.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:23.660
When New Hampshire crews chose salt in 1938, they optimized for one measurable outcome,

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.840
fewer winter crashes, without a parallel measurement for what the chloride would do once it left

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:29.420
the pavement.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:34.780
Any maintenance rule that rewards only immediate road condition will keep externalizing costs

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:38.020
into water bodies whose turnover time is measured in decades.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:44.340
Agencies that now require both crash data and annual salt load accounting per watershed are

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.380
beginning to internalize that longer loop.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.040
The slow chemistry of a watershed is the kind of record environmental intelligence

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:54.680
follows when a short safety metric outruns the water it lands in.

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:59.500
How might other seasonal safety decisions change if their material residue were tracked

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.100
on the same time scale as the accidents they prevent?

